The fix for #531 is committed (0ec824b7) and queued to land on main, but the land hadn't run yet when my time ran out, so #531 is still open. The manager lands the commit after this run, and the next run posts the final comment and closes the issue.

**What was wrong:** the issue named the cert-stream fixture, but five Rust test fixtures had the same race: cert-stream, external-token, unary-retry, and the two w2s-validate tests. Each one bound port 0, read the port number, released it, and then had its server bind that port again. Another test running in parallel could take the port in between.

**What changed:**
- Each of the five fixtures now keeps the listener it bound (`TcpIncoming` on `127.0.0.1:0`) and hands it to `serve_with_incoming`, the way the stream-expiry fixture already does.
- A new test, `scripts/cross_test_listener_test.go`, fails on any cross-test Rust file that binds port 0 and then drops the listener. It failed on main for all five files and passes with the fix.

**Verification:**
- `just cert-stream-rust`, `external-token-rust`, `w2s-validate-rust` and `unary-retry-rust` all pass, and so does `go test ./scripts/`.
- Running under parallel load (the issue's "show it holds" ask): the cert-stream Rust suite passed 50 out of 50 runs at 48 threads.
- The old fixture also passed 50 out of 50, so the race is too rare on this runner to reproduce. The new guard test is the reliable check, not the stress run.
- I did not run `just gates` locally; the land runs the gates.

**Patch release:** main has taken features since v2.6.1, so per the release rule I tagged nothing on main, including the `v2.6.2` the run instructions named. I opened backport pull requests #532–#538 for release/2.0 through 2.6. Each names the tag to cut once it merges (v2.0.27 … v2.6.9). The guard test passes on all seven branches. I ran the four Rust test suites only on the 2.0, 2.3 and 2.6 backports, and they pass there.

**Trace check:** the handlers I changed are test servers that make no outgoing calls. `looper-trace check` reports failures only in files this change doesn't touch, and those are already filed as #310 and #311.

I posted a progress comment on #531 with these details and checkpointed the branch.
